---
title: Proposition 16
---
order: 16
---
short_label: X.16
---
sections:

#### prop_section ####
kind: proposition
----
statement: If two incommensurable magnitudes are added together, the sum is also incommensurable with each of them; but, if the sum is incommensurable with one of them, then the original magnitudes are also incommensurable.
----
proof:

Let the two incommensurable magnitudes {AB} and {BC} be added together.

I say that the whole {AC} is also incommensurable with each of the magnitudes {AB} and {BC}.

For, if {CA} and {AB} are not incommensurable, then some magnitude {D} measures them.

